title: Read receipts
pid: 1e1892ad-7198-425b-9420-98f887c5623f
body: |2-
  Mail between two people runs through forks: each has a note
  addressed to the other, a /mailbox/, and the reader holds a fork of
  it, whose forks folder shows what the mailbox holds that the reader
  has neither filed nor hidden: the unread items. A /read receipt/
  says how the reader handled an item and files it away, in one
  gesture: =skg-receipt= (=C-c m a=, on the item's headline; in Neovim
  =<localleader>ma=, =:SkgReceipt=; in skg-for-ai =receipt ITEM TEXT=).
  It asks for one line, how the item was handled (="Read."= by
  default), and then, in one write:

  - writes a new note, titled =Read.=, its body that line (none when
    the line is the title), as the first child of the reader's fork of
    the item: the fork the reader holds, else a new one, made as an
    edit of the item would make it, and with no confirmation, since
    the command asked for it. The author sees the receipt as
    unintegrated content under their item;
  - hides the item from the reader's fork of the mailbox, as removing
    its headline there would, so it leaves the unread items.

  The mailbox is a note of another owner that holds the item, or a
  mirror of it, and that a note of the reader's forks. Where the item
  is a mirror (the author's reply, forked from the reader's own
  message, say), the hide lands on the member the mailbox's list
  names, as a save's would ("Editing the clone"). With one mailbox it
  is found; with several the command asks which. With none the
  receipt is written and nothing hidden, and so where the fork of the
  mailbox already hides the item. An item the reader owns takes the
  receipt itself.

  A new fork goes into the skgrepo of the reader's fork of the
  mailbox, but only where the item is homed in the mailbox's own
  skgrepo: an item from another of its owner's skgrepos may be more
  private than the mailbox, and so than the reader's fork of it, which
  may be public. Then, and for an item in no mailbox, the command asks
  for the skgrepo, unless the reader owns only one the skgrepo
  restriction shows.

  The server does it all ('server/receipt.rs'), as the request
  "receipt" ('docs/api-and-formats.org'): the receipt, the fork and
  the fork of the mailbox are written as one batch, then the views
  that show them are redrawn, but those with unsaved edits.
